OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026", "title": "A Conditional Reduction for Algebraic Kuga–Satake Correspondences", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Conditional-Reduction-for-Algebraic-Kuga-Satake-Correspondences-September-10-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "032", "family_title": "The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties", "abstract_html": "For a polarized K3 surface whose primitive cohomology has full orthogonal Hodge group, one algebraic correspondence inducing a nonzero map from that cohomology to the second cohomology of an abelian variety suffices to recover the prescribed full Kuga–Satake correspondence. We give an equivalent condition using holomorphic one-forms on a generically finite surface cover. If this input holds very generally in a polarized component, the prescribed correspondence is algebraic throughout that component, for every choice of standard data on the transcendental part. The existence of the initial correspondence remains a hypothesis.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Recovery at a point with full Hodge group", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">All varieties are over \\(\\mathbb C\\), and cohomology is rational Betti cohomology unless another field is displayed. Write \\(\\mathrm{CH}^r(-)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) for codimension-\\(r\\) cycles modulo rational equivalence with rational coefficients, and \\(\\mathrm{CH}_a\\) for cycles of dimension \\(a\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The Tate twist \\((1)\\) shifts Hodge bidegrees by \\((-1,-1)\\). On a Hodge component of type \\((p,q)\\), the Hodge circle acts as \\(e^{i(p-q)\\theta}\\). The joint Hodge group of several structures is the smallest rational algebraic group containing their simultaneous Hodge circle.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\((S,h)\\) be a primitively amply polarized K3 surface, with \\(h^2=2d\\). For the cup form \\(q\\), put\n  \\[\n  V=h^\\perp\\subset H^2(S,\\mathbb Q),\n  \\qquad\n  T=T(S)=\\mathrm{NS}(S)_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\perp}\\subset V.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(\\dim V=21\\), \\(q|_V\\) has signature \\((2,19)\\), and \\(T\\) contains the period plane and has dimension at least two.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  \\mathrm{Hg}(V)=\\mathrm{SO}(V,q).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a rational quadratic polarization \\(Q\\) of \\(T\\) and all stipulated standard Kuga–Satake data: the full even Clifford construction, its parity identification by right multiplication by a fixed invertible odd Clifford element, a rational weight-one polarization, and a chosen isogenous realization \\(A_S\\). Put \\(W_S=H^1(A_S,\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The standard map \\(T(1)\\to\\mathrm{End}(W_S)\\), together with the stipulated identification \\(W_S^\\vee\\simeq W_S(1)\\), determines\n  \\[\n  \\kappa_S:T\\longrightarrow W_S\\otimes W_S\n  \\hookrightarrow H^2(A_S\\times A_S,\\mathbb Q).\n  \\tag{1.1}\n  \\]\n  The last arrow is the ordered Künneth inclusion. Algebraic realization means the existence of\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma_S\\in\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times A_S\\times A_S)_{\\mathbb Q}\n  \\]\n  such that\n  \\[\n  (p_{AA})_*\\bigl(\\mathrm{cl}(\\Gamma_S)\\cup p_S^*x\\bigr)\n  =\\kappa_S(x)\n  \\qquad(x\\in T).\n  \\tag{1.2}\n  \\]\n  Every normalization in \\((1.1)\\) is retained; the full target is not replaced by a simple spin factor.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following conditions are equivalent.</p><p>(i) There are an abelian variety \\(B\\) and a cycle\n\\[\n\\alpha\\in\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times B)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\alpha_*:V\\longrightarrow H^2(B,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nis nonzero.</p><p>(ii) There are a smooth connected projective surface \\(Y\\) and a dominant generically finite morphism \\(f:Y\\to S\\) such that\n\\[\nf_*\\left(\n\\operatorname{im}\\left[\n\\bigwedge\\nolimits^2H^0(Y,\\Omega_Y^1)\n\\longrightarrow H^0(Y,\\Omega_Y^2)\n\\right]\\right)\\ne0.\n\\tag{1.3}\n\\]\nHere \\(f_*\\) is the trace on holomorphic two-forms, equivalently cohomological pushforward restricted to \\(H^{2,0}\\).</p><p>(iii) The prescribed full Kuga–Satake map is algebraically realized as in \\((1.2)\\), for any stipulated standard data on \\(T\\).</p><p>In this situation \\(T=V\\). The same input realizes the standard map for the full primitive Clifford construction with the cup form. There is no genericity condition on \\(B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 gives \\(T=V\\). Proposition 2 proves the equivalence of conditions (i) and (ii).</p><p>From condition (i), Proposition 6 realizes the full primitive Clifford multiplication for the cup form. Proposition 11 changes its quadratic polarization to the stipulated one, and Proposition 4 supplies the prescribed parity identification, duality, rational normalization, and isogenous abelian realization. This gives condition (iii).</p><p>Conversely, condition (iii) gives condition (i) by taking\n\\[\nB=A_S\\times A_S,\n\\qquad\n\\alpha=\\Gamma_S.\n\\]\nThe standard Clifford map is an embedding, so its restriction to \\(V=T\\) is nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Specialization in a polarized component", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a component of the moduli space of primitively amply polarized K3 surfaces of degree \\(2d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\((S,h)\\), define \\(V=h^\\perp\\), \\(T=\\mathrm{NS}(S)_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\perp}\\), and the prescribed map \\(\\kappa_S\\) as in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that condition (i) or condition (ii) of Theorem 1 holds very generally in this component.</p><p class=\"li\">It suffices that, about each desired point, the input holds outside a countable union of proper closed analytic subsets of a full polarized period chart. In particular, an input outside countably many proper algebraic subsets of the moduli component suffices.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\((S,h)\\) in the component admits a cycle\n\\[\n\\Gamma_S\\in\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times A_S\\times A_S)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nwhose action on \\(T(S)\\) is exactly \\(\\kappa_S\\), with all stipulated standard data.</p><p>The witness cycles, abelian varieties, or surface covers need not vary in a family. The hypothesis concerns actual algebraic cycles; absolute Hodge or motivated classes do not supply this input.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 2, either assumed input gives the nonzero algebraic correspondence input very generally.</p><p>Proposition 9 algebraically realizes the full primitive multiplication \\(m^q\\) on \\(V\\) at the desired point. Proposition 10 restricts it to \\(m^{q|_T}\\) on \\(T\\). Proposition 11 changes to the prescribed rational quadratic polarization on \\(T\\). Proposition 4 then supplies the prescribed right multiplier, duality, rational normalization, and identification with the actual \\(H^1(A_S,\\mathbb Q)\\) model.</p><p>Applying the target Künneth projectors yields\n\\[\n\\Gamma_S\\in\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times A_S\\times A_S)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n(\\Gamma_S)_*x=\\kappa_S(x)\n\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in T.\n\\]\nAll abelian-factor operations preserve degree shift, so the resulting cycle has codimension two.</p><p>This includes Picard jumps, extra real or complex multiplication, and the smallest transcendental ranks. It realizes the prescribed multiplication tensor at those points and does not assert generation of every Hodge embedding for a smaller special Hodge group. Producing the very-general nonzero algebraic correspondence, or the equivalent surface cover, remains the external existence hypothesis.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 1.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Assume that the hypothesis of Theorem 2 holds in the required polarized components in every degree.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every projective K3 surface \\(S\\), and for all stipulated standard Kuga–Satake data on \\(T(S)\\), the prescribed map \\(\\kappa_S\\) is realized by a rational codimension-two algebraic cycle as in \\((1.2)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Every projective K3 surface has an ample integral class. Divide it to primitivity in \\(H^2(S,\\mathbb Z)\\). The resulting integral \\((1,1)\\)-class is the first Chern class of a line bundle, a positive power of which is ample. Hence the line bundle itself is ample, and its square is a positive even integer.</p><p>Apply Theorem 2 in the corresponding polarized component.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Cycle operations and rational descent.", "statement": "<p>Cycle operations and rational descent.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For smooth projective varieties \\(P,Q\\), a correspondence \\(Z\\in\\mathrm{CH}^a(P\\times Q)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) acts by\n  \\[\n  Z_*x=(p_Q)_*\\bigl(\\mathrm{cl}(Z)\\cup p_P^*x\\bigr):\n  H^j(P)\\longrightarrow H^{j+2e}(Q)(e),\n  \\qquad e=a-\\dim P.\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Composition through a variety of dimension \\(r\\) adds codimensions and subtracts \\(r\\). Cycle classes act compatibly with composition.</p><p class=\"li\">An ordered tensor product of first cohomology groups is regarded as its ordered Künneth component.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Rational weight-one Hodge maps on abelian varieties can be applied algebraically and independently to the two tensor factors. The relevant degree and mixed Künneth projectors are algebraic. Membership of a rational cohomological map in the span of rational cycle actions descends from any extension field to \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p>These operations preserve degree shift. In particular, degree-preserving correspondences from a surface to degree-two cohomology have codimension two.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 realizes independent rational weight-one maps by abelian correspondences. Lemma 1.2 supplies algebraic degree projectors and the mixed-component injection. Lemma 1.3 gives rational descent of the resulting cycle span.</p><p>The composition rule in \\((2.1)\\) shows that these degree-zero target operations preserve the degree shift of a correspondence from the surface.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Weight-one operations", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(P,Q\\) be complex abelian varieties.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Pullback identifies\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hom}(Q,P)\\otimes\\mathbb Q\n\\simeq\n\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}\n\\bigl(H^1(P,\\mathbb Q),H^1(Q,\\mathbb Q)\\bigr).\n\\]\nConsequently, arbitrary rational weight-one Hodge maps can act algebraically and independently on the two factors of an ordered tensor product of first cohomology groups. Preservation of chosen polarizations is not required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Dualize a rational Hodge map to a map on first homology. After multiplication by a positive integer it preserves the integral lattices. The Hodge condition says that its real extension commutes with the complex structures on the universal covers. It therefore induces a holomorphic group homomorphism \\(Q\\to P\\), which is algebraic by projectivity. Conversely, pullback by every such homomorphism is a Hodge map.</p><p>This is the usual equivalence between abelian varieties up to isogeny and polarizable rational Hodge structures of weight one, written contravariantly [13, Theorem 6.8 and Corollary 6.9]. Transpose graphs realize the pullbacks. Products of two such correspondences, and their rational linear combinations, act independently on the two tensor factors.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Degree projectors", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(B\\) be a complex abelian variety, let \\(U=H^1(B,\\mathbb Q)\\), and let \\(m:B\\times B\\to B\\) be addition.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every cohomological Künneth projector on a product of abelian varieties is induced by a rational algebraic correspondence. The mixed part of \\(m^*\\) gives an injection\n\\[\nH^2(B,\\mathbb Q)=\\bigwedge\\nolimits^2U\n\\longrightarrow U\\otimes U,\n\\qquad\nu\\wedge v\\longmapsto u\\otimes v-v\\otimes u.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For an abelian variety \\(B\\) of dimension \\(b\\), multiplication by \\(2\\) acts on \\(H^j(B)\\) by \\(2^j\\). Thus the rational polynomial\n\\[\ne_j(T)=\n\\prod_{\\substack{0\\le k\\le2b\\\\k\\ne j}}\n\\frac{T-2^k}{2^j-2^k}\n\\]\napplied to the correspondence \\([2]^*\\) is the projector onto \\(H^j(B)\\). Apply these polynomials separately to each factor of a product.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nm^*u=p_1^*u+p_2^*u\n\\]\non \\(H^1\\). Multiplication in the exterior algebra, with its usual signs, gives the displayed mixed-component formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Rational descent of a cycle span", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(E\\) be a finite-dimensional rational vector space of cohomological maps, and let \\(E_{\\mathrm{alg}}\\subset E\\) be the image of rational algebraic correspondences of a fixed degree.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every field extension \\(K/\\mathbb Q\\),\n\\[\nE\\cap(E_{\\mathrm{alg}}\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}K)=E_{\\mathrm{alg}}\n\\qquad\\text{inside }E\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}K.\n\\]\nThe same assertion holds if only a specified rational subspace of the source is considered.</p>", "proof": "<p>The cycle class map and restriction of its action are rational linear maps, so \\(E_{\\mathrm{alg}}\\) is a rational subspace.</p><p>If a rational target lies in the scalar-extended span of finitely many rational cycle actions, write these actions as columns in a rational basis of \\(E\\). The linear system with that target as right-hand side is consistent over \\(K\\), and row reduction over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) gives a rational solution. Only finitely many cycles are used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Correspondences and surface covers", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\((S,h)\\) be a primitively amply polarized K3 surface and put \\(V=h^\\perp\\subset H^2(S,\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p>Consider:</p><p class=\"li\">the correspondence condition: there are an abelian variety \\(B\\) and \\(\\alpha\\in\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times B)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) with \\(\\alpha_*|_V\\ne0\\);</p><p class=\"li\">the surface condition: there are a smooth connected projective surface \\(Y\\) and a dominant generically finite \\(f:Y\\to S\\) satisfying \\((1.3)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The surface condition implies the correspondence condition without a full Hodge-group hypothesis. If, in addition,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hg}(V)=\\mathrm{SO}(V,q),\n\\]\nthen the correspondence condition implies the surface condition. Thus under this additional hypothesis the two conditions are equivalent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose first that \\(f:Y\\to S\\) satisfies \\((1.3)\\). Let \\(D=\\mathrm{Alb}(Y)\\), let \\(a:Y\\to D\\) be an Albanese map, and put \\(b=\\dim D\\). Albanese pullback identifies holomorphic one-forms [3, Chapter VI, §9.2]. Hence there is\n\\[\n\\beta\\in H^{2,0}(D)=\\bigwedge\\nolimits^2H^{1,0}(D),\n\\qquad\ny=f_*a^*\\beta\\ne0.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(y\\in H^{2,0}(S)\\subset V_{\\mathbb C}\\).</p><p>The surface correspondence \\((a,f)_*[Y]\\) has codimension \\(b\\) on \\(D\\times S\\). Its transpose acts by\n\\[\na_*f^*:H^2(S)\\longrightarrow H^{2b-2}(D)(b-2).\n\\]\nThis action is nonzero on \\(V\\): the projection formula gives\n\\[\n\\int_D\\beta\\cup a_*f^*\\overline y\n=\n\\int_Ya^*\\beta\\cup f^*\\overline y\n=\n\\int_Sy\\cup\\overline y\n\\ne0.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]</p><p>For an ample line bundle \\(L\\) on \\(D\\), written additively, set\n\\[\nE=m^*L-p_1^*L-p_2^*L\\in\\mathrm{CH}^1(D\\times D),\n\\]\nwhere \\(m\\) is addition. The class of \\(E\\) is purely mixed. Put \\(U=H^1(D,\\mathbb Q)\\) and write\n\\[\n\\mathrm{cl}(E)=\\sum_i u_i\\otimes v_i\n\\]\nwith \\((u_i)\\) a basis of \\(U\\). Its matrix\n\\[\nM:U^\\vee\\longrightarrow U,\n\\qquad\nM(\\varphi)=\\sum_i\\varphi(u_i)v_i\n\\]\nis invertible: it is the alternating tensor associated with \\(c_1(L)\\), whose top exterior power is nonzero since \\(L\\) is ample. Intrinsically,\n\\[\nM:U^\\vee\\longrightarrow U(1)\n\\]\nis a Hodge map; the matrix calculation uses underlying rational spaces.</p><p>With the Koszul convention for Künneth products,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{cl}(E)^2\n=\n-2\\sum_{i&lt;j}(u_i\\wedge u_j)\\otimes(v_i\\wedge v_j).\n\\]\nPoincaré duality identifies \\(H^{2b-2}(D)\\) with \\((\\bigwedge^2U)^\\vee\\) by integration against degree-two classes. Consequently \\((E^2)_*\\) is \\(-2\\bigwedge^2M\\) after this identification, and is an isomorphism\n\\[\n(E^2)_*:H^{2b-2}(D)(b-2)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H^2(D).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nThe twists can also be checked from\n\\[\nH^{2b-2}(D)(b-2)\\simeq H^2(D)^\\vee(-2),\n\\qquad\n\\bigwedge\\nolimits^2M:H^2(D)^\\vee\\longrightarrow H^2(D)(2).\n\\]\nComposing with \\((f,a)_*[Y]\\) yields a cycle of codimension \\(b+2-b=2\\) on \\(S\\times D\\), nonzero on \\(V\\) by \\((2.2)\\). This uses a square of an explicit divisor and assumes no algebraic inverse Lefschetz operator for a general variety.</p><p>Conversely, suppose \\(\\alpha_*:V\\to H^2(B)\\) is nonzero and \\(\\mathrm{Hg}(V)=\\mathrm{SO}(V,q)\\). The standard representation is irreducible, so \\(\\alpha_*|_V\\) is injective. For \\(0\\ne\\sigma\\in H^{2,0}(S)\\), put\n\\[\n\\eta=\\alpha_*\\sigma\\ne0\\in H^{2,0}(B).\n\\]\nWrite \\(c=\\dim B\\ge2\\), and let \\(\\ell\\) be an ample divisor class on \\(B\\), replaced by a very ample multiple if necessary. The rational surface cycle\n\\[\nC=\\alpha\\cdot p_B^*\\ell^{c-2}\\in\\mathrm{CH}_2(S\\times B)_{\\mathbb Q}\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n\\int_{S\\times B}\\mathrm{cl}(C)\\cup p_S^*\\overline\\sigma\\cup p_B^*\\eta\n=\n\\int_B\\overline\\eta\\cup\\eta\\cup\\ell^{c-2}\n\\ne0.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nHere rationality gives \\(\\alpha_*\\overline\\sigma=\\overline\\eta\\), and the last pairing is definite up to a fixed nonzero sign on \\(H^{2,0}(B)\\) by the Hodge–Riemann bilinear relations.</p><p>An actual representative of \\(C\\) is a finite rational combination of integral surfaces; general hyperplanes may be used to represent the indicated intersections. At least one component has nonzero pairing in \\((2.4)\\). Resolve it projectively and call the resulting smooth connected surface \\(Y\\). Its projections give morphisms\n\\[\nf:Y\\to S,\n\\qquad\na':Y\\to B\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n\\int_Yf^*\\overline\\sigma\\cup a'^*\\eta\\ne0.\n\\]\nThus \\(f^*\\sigma\\ne0\\), which forces \\(f\\) to have two-dimensional image. It is therefore dominant and generically finite. The projection formula also gives \\(f_*a'^*\\eta\\ne0\\). Since \\(\\eta\\) is a sum of wedges of holomorphic one-forms on \\(B\\), its pullback lies in the wedge image in \\((1.3)\\).</p><p>As noted in original Remark 2.5, the reverse implication uses injectivity on \\(V\\), not merely nonvanishing on a divisor class. At a Picard jump, a correspondence nonzero only on new algebraic classes need not satisfy the surface condition.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Full Clifford Hodge structures", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a rational Hodge structure of K3 type: it has weight two, \\(h^{2,0}=h^{0,2}=1\\), and only Hodge types \\((2,0),(1,1),(0,2)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Q\\) be a nondegenerate rational quadratic Hodge form. Allow either overall sign: \\(Q\\) has sign \\(\\epsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\) on the real period plane and the opposite sign on its orthogonal.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  C(H,Q)=C^+(H,Q)\\oplus C^-(H,Q),\n  \\qquad v^2=Q(v,v).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">If\n  \\[\n  H^{2,0}=\\mathbb C(e_1+ie_2),\n  \\qquad Q(e_j,e_j)=\\epsilon,\n  \\qquad Q(e_1,e_2)=0,\n  \\]\n  set\n  \\[\n  J=\\epsilon e_1e_2,\n  \\qquad\n  g_\\theta=\\cos\\theta+J\\sin\\theta.\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Left multiplication by \\(g_\\theta\\) gives both parity spaces weight-one Hodge structures \\(P_H^+\\) and \\(P_H^-\\), with the \\(i\\)-eigenspace of \\(L_J\\) as their \\((1,0)\\)-part. These are the full parity spaces.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Hodge structures \\(P_H^\\pm\\) are polarizable, and\n\\[\nm^Q:H(1)\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{Hom}(P_H^+,P_H^-),\n\\qquad x\\longmapsto L_x\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nis a rational Hodge embedding.</p><p>Right multiplication by any fixed invertible odd Clifford element gives a rational Hodge isomorphism between the two parity spaces. On a sufficiently small period chart, the Hodge filtrations vary holomorphically and admit fixed rational polarizations.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Clifford relations give\n\\[\nJ^2=-1,\n\\qquad\n[J,e_1]=-2e_2,\n\\qquad\n[J,e_2]=2e_1,\n\\qquad\n[J,e_1+ie_2]=2i(e_1+ie_2).\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(J\\) commutes with the orthogonal complement of the period plane. Thus \\(g_\\theta\\in\\mathrm{Spin}(H,Q)(\\mathbb R)\\), and its conjugation action on \\(H\\) is the prescribed weight-two Hodge circle. Since\n\\[\nL_{g_\\theta}L_xL_{g_\\theta}^{-1}\n=\nL_{g_\\theta xg_\\theta^{-1}},\n\\]\nthe map in \\((3.2)\\) is a Hodge map. It is injective because \\(L_x(1)=x\\).</p><p>Choose a rational nonisotropic vector \\(z\\in H\\). Right multiplication \\(R_z\\) exchanges the parities, has inverse \\(R_z/Q(z,z)\\), and commutes with the left circle. The same observation applies to every invertible odd right multiplier.</p><p>For holomorphic variation, put \\(\\omega=e_1+ie_2\\). The relation \\(J\\omega=i\\omega\\) shows that the image of \\(L_\\omega\\) from the opposite parity is contained in the \\(i\\)-eigenspace of \\(L_J\\). Conversely, \\(\\omega J=-i\\omega\\), so this eigenspace is contained in \\(\\ker L_\\omega\\). Finally,\n\\[\nL_\\omega L_{\\overline\\omega}+L_{\\overline\\omega}L_\\omega\n=\n4\\epsilon\\,\\mathrm{id}\n\\]\nshows that \\(\\ker L_\\omega=\\operatorname{im}L_\\omega\\), with the appropriate parities understood. Hence\n\\[\nF^1P_{H,\\mathbb C}^\\pm=\\operatorname{im}L_\\omega,\n\\]\na holomorphic subbundle as the isotropic line varies.</p><p>For a fixed rational polarization, let \\(u\\mapsto u^*\\) denote reversal in the Clifford algebra and \\(\\operatorname{scal}\\) its scalar part. Choose rational orthogonal vectors \\(f_1,f_2\\) of sign \\(\\epsilon\\) whose oriented plane is sufficiently close to the given period plane. Rational approximation followed by rational orthogonalization supplies them. Put \\(a=f_1f_2\\) and, on \\(C^+(H,Q)\\), define\n\\[\ns(u,v)=\\operatorname{scal}(uav^*).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nThe scalar part is cyclic and invariant under reversal, as checked on an orthogonal monomial basis. Since \\(a^*=-a\\), the form \\(s\\) is alternating. It is nondegenerate because \\(a\\) is invertible and \\((u,v)\\mapsto\\operatorname{scal}(uv^*)\\) is nondegenerate. Reversal gives \\(g_\\theta^*g_\\theta=1\\), so \\(s\\) is invariant under the left circle at every period.</p><p>At the reference plane spanned by \\(f_1,f_2\\), write \\(a=cJ\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb R^\\times\\). Then\n\\[\ns(u,Jv)=c\\,\\operatorname{scal}\\bigl((\\operatorname{Ad}(J)u)v^*\\bigr).\n\\]\nExtend a normalized basis of this plane to an orthogonal basis with squared lengths\n\\[\n\\epsilon,\\epsilon,-\\epsilon,\\ldots,-\\epsilon.\n\\]\nThe last pairing is diagonal on even monomials \\(e_I\\). If \\(r=|I|\\) and \\(k\\) entries of \\(I\\) belong to the period plane, its diagonal sign is\n\\[\n(-1)^k\\epsilon^r(-1)^{r-k}=1,\n\\]\nbecause \\(r\\) is even. Up to the overall sign required by the weight-one polarization convention, \\(s\\) is therefore a polarization.</p><p>Circle invariance makes \\(s(u,Jv)\\) symmetric and nondegenerate at every period. Its signature is constant on a connected period chart containing the reference plane and the original period. The chosen sign of \\(s\\) therefore polarizes throughout that chart. Transport by \\(R_z\\) supplies a fixed polarization on \\(C^-(H,Q)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Operations on the two slots and the exact even-Clifford target", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\subset H^2(S,\\mathbb Q)\\), where \\(S\\) is a smooth projective surface.</p><p class=\"li\">For each polarizable rational weight-one Hodge structure \\(P\\), fix the polarization-induced rational Hodge isomorphism\n  \\[\n  j_P:P^\\vee(-1)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}P.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A Hodge map \\(f:H(1)\\to\\mathrm{Hom}(P,M)\\) determines\n  \\[\n  H\\longrightarrow\n  \\mathrm{Hom}(P,M)(-1)\n  =\n  M\\otimes P^\\vee(-1)\n  \\xrightarrow{1\\otimes j_P}M\\otimes P.\n  \\tag{3.4}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose abelian realizations \\(M=H^1(A_M,\\mathbb Q)\\) and \\(P=H^1(A_P,\\mathbb Q)\\). Say that \\(f\\) is algebraically realized if a cycle in \\(\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times A_M\\times A_P)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) has exactly the action \\((3.4)\\) on \\(H\\), included in the ordered \\(H^1\\otimes H^1\\) Künneth component.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(f\\) is algebraically realized.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For rational weight-one Hodge maps\n\\[\na:M\\to M',\n\\qquad\nb:P'\\to P,\n\\]\nthe map \\(x\\mapsto af(x)b\\) is algebraically realized. The same holds after changing chosen duality isomorphisms, interchanging tensor factors, or making rational weight-one Hodge identifications with isogenous abelian models.</p><p>In particular, for \\(W=P_H^+=C^+(H,Q)\\) and a stipulated invertible odd right multiplier \\(z\\), algebraicity of \\(m^Q\\) gives algebraicity of the precise prescribed map\n\\[\nH(1)\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{End}(W),\n\\qquad\nx\\longmapsto\\bigl[w\\longmapsto xwz\\bigr]\n=\nR_zL_x,\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nand hence of its tensor realization using the stipulated duality.</p>", "proof": "<p>Under \\((3.4)\\), the two operations are precisely\n\\[\na\\otimes\\bigl(j_{P'}b^\\vee(-1)j_P^{-1}\\bigr).\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nBoth factors are rational weight-one Hodge maps, so Proposition 1 realizes them by abelian correspondences. Their product acts independently on the two factors and preserves the codimension of the correspondence from the surface.</p><p>Changing \\(j_P\\) likewise composes with a weight-one Hodge isomorphism. The factor-switching isomorphism is algebraic; its Künneth sign can be included in the rational coefficient. The degree projectors of Proposition 1 remove all other Künneth components when necessary.</p><p>For the standard full even-Clifford model, compose \\(m^Q\\) on its output Hom slot with \\(R_z:P_H^-\\to P_H^+\\). This gives exactly \\((3.6)\\). Applying the stipulated duality \\(W^\\vee\\simeq W(1)\\) yields the specified map to \\(W\\otimes W\\), with the fixed multiplier, polarization, rational normalization, and identification with the first cohomology of the chosen Kuga–Satake abelian variety. No simple factor replaces the full even-Clifford space.</p><p>The usual equivalent Clifford conventions require only these operations. Reversal takes left multiplication by a spin element to right multiplication by its inverse:\n\\[\n(gu)^*=u^*g^{-1}.\n\\]\nPassing between homology and cohomology uses duality, transpose, and a weight-one polarization. Reversing the period Hodge action independently would be a different construction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "The generic Hodge group", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(D\\) be a sufficiently small, full-dimensional marked polarized period chart for primitively amply polarized K3 surfaces. The marking identifies the primitive rational cohomology with a fixed quadratic space \\((V,q)\\) of dimension \\(21\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Outside a countable union of proper closed analytic subsets of \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hg}(V)=\\mathrm{SO}(V,q).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nAt every point satisfying \\((4.1)\\), \\(V\\) is an irreducible rational Hodge structure and \\(T(S)=V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Polarized local Torelli identifies the chart with an open subset of the domain of oriented positive two-planes in \\(V_{\\mathbb R}\\).</p><p>The infinitesimal generators of the Hodge circles over any such open subset span \\(\\mathfrak{so}(V_{\\mathbb R},q)\\). Indeed, under\n\\[\n\\mathfrak{so}(V_{\\mathbb R},q)\\simeq\\bigwedge\\nolimits^2V_{\\mathbb R},\n\\]\neach generator is a nonzero multiple of the bivector of its positive plane. A linear functional vanishing on all these bivectors is an alternating bilinear form vanishing on an open set of ordered pairs of vectors. It therefore vanishes identically.</p><p>The Hodge group is a connected rational algebraic subgroup of \\(\\mathrm{SO}(V,q)\\). Its rational identity component contains the connected Hodge circle and hence, by minimality, is the whole group.</p><p>If this subgroup is proper, its Lie algebra is a nonzero proper rational subspace\n\\[\nL\\subset\\mathfrak{so}(V,q).\n\\]\nSet \\(r=\\dim L\\), and choose a rational generator \\(\\tau_L\\) of \\(\\bigwedge^rL\\). Conjugation by the circle preserves \\(L\\) and acts trivially on its real determinant line: a continuous character from a compact connected circle to \\(\\mathbb R^\\times\\) is trivial. Thus \\(\\tau_L\\), viewed in \\(\\bigwedge^r\\mathrm{End}(V)\\), has Hodge type \\((0,0)\\).</p><p>There are countably many possible rational subspaces \\(L\\). For each, the condition that \\(\\tau_L\\) have type \\((0,0)\\) is closed analytic. The tensor has weight zero and is real, so this is equivalent to \\(\\tau_L\\in F^0\\), a holomorphic condition.</p><p>The locus is proper. Otherwise \\(L_{\\mathbb R}\\) would be preserved by every circle in the chart, hence by the adjoint actions of their infinitesimal generators. The spanning assertion would make \\(L_{\\mathbb C}\\) an ideal of the simple Lie algebra \\(\\mathfrak{so}_{21}(\\mathbb C)\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Finally, the standard representation of \\(\\mathrm{SO}(V,q)\\) is absolutely irreducible and has no nonzero invariants. Consequently \\(V\\) has no proper nonzero rational Hodge substructure and no nonzero rational vector of type \\((1,1)\\). Since the primitive part of \\(\\mathrm{NS}(S)_{\\mathbb Q}\\) consists of such vectors,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NS}(S)_{\\mathbb Q}=\\mathbb Qh,\n\\qquad\nT(S)=V.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Recovery of every rational Hodge map into the full Clifford tensor target", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((S,h)\\) be a primitively amply polarized K3 surface,\n  \\[\n  V=h^\\perp,\\qquad \\dim V=21,\n  \\qquad \\mathrm{Hg}(V)=\\mathrm{SO}(V,q).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a complex abelian variety and suppose\n  \\[\n  \\alpha\\in\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times B)_{\\mathbb Q}\n  \\]\n  induces a nonzero map \\(V\\to H^2(B,\\mathbb Q)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  U=H^1(B,\\mathbb Q),\n  \\qquad\n  W^+=C^+(V,q),\n  \\qquad\n  W^-=C^-(V,q),\n  \\]\n  with the full weight-one Hodge structures of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2\\in\\{+,-\\}\\), and abelian varieties \\(A_1,A_2\\) realizing \\(W^{\\epsilon_1},W^{\\epsilon_2}\\) as their first rational cohomology.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every rational Hodge map\n\\[\n\\beta:V\\longrightarrow W^{\\epsilon_1}\\otimes W^{\\epsilon_2}\n\\subset H^2(A_1\\times A_2,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nis the restriction of the action of a cycle\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\in\\mathrm{CH}^2(S\\times A_1\\times A_2)_{\\mathbb Q}.\n\\]\nIn particular, the input recovers the full primitive Clifford multiplication tensor and its prescribed even-Clifford realization.</p>", "proof": "<p>Pull back \\(\\alpha\\) along addition \\(B\\times B\\to B\\), and apply the algebraic projector onto \\(U\\otimes U\\subset H^2(B\\times B,\\mathbb Q)\\) from Proposition 1. The resulting codimension-two cycle induces\n\\[\n\\alpha':V\\longrightarrow U\\otimes U.\n\\]\nIt is nonzero because the mixed component of addition pullback is the injection\n\\[\nu\\wedge v\\longmapsto u\\otimes v-v\\otimes u.\n\\]</p><p>Fix a nonzero \\(\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}\\)-equivariant map\n\\[\n\\gamma:V_{\\mathbb C}\\to\\Delta\\otimes\\Delta.\n\\]\nThe map \\(\\alpha'\\) is rational and Hodge, hence \\(\\mathfrak g\\)-equivariant. Lemmas 6.2 and 6.3 give, after regrouping the spin factors first,\n\\[\n\\alpha'_{\\mathbb C}=\\gamma\\otimes t,\n\\qquad\n0\\ne t\\in M\\otimes M.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nThe other summands of \\(U_{\\mathbb C}\\otimes U_{\\mathbb C}\\) contain no vector copy.</p><p>Put \\(N_j=N_{\\epsilon_j}\\) for \\(j=1,2\\). Independent operations\n\\[\na\\in\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(M,N_1),\n\\qquad\nb\\in\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(M,N_2)\n\\]\nsend \\(t\\) to \\((a\\otimes b)t\\), and these images span \\(N_1\\otimes N_2\\). Indeed, choose \\(\\mu,\\nu\\in M^\\vee\\) with\n\\[\nc=(\\mu\\otimes\\nu)(t)\\ne0.\n\\]\nFor arbitrary \\(n_1\\in N_1,n_2\\in N_2\\), the maps\n\\[\na(m)=\\mu(m)n_1,\n\\qquad\nb(m)=\\nu(m)n_2\n\\]\nsend \\(t\\) to \\(c\\,n_1\\otimes n_2\\). Thus symmetry imposed by the original exterior square causes no restriction.</p><p>Choose rational bases\n\\[\nf_1,\\ldots,f_r\n\\quad\\text{of }\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathfrak g}(U,W^{\\epsilon_1}),\n\\qquad\ng_1,\\ldots,g_s\n\\quad\\text{of }\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathfrak g}(U,W^{\\epsilon_2}).\n\\]\nBy Lemma 6.2 these are Hodge maps, and their complex spans give all the independent operations above. Proposition 1 realizes each by a rational abelian homomorphism acting contravariantly on \\(H^1\\). Products of the transpose-graph correspondences, followed by the mixed projector if necessary, realize\n\\[\n\\Phi_{ij}=(f_i\\otimes g_j)\\alpha'\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nby rational cycles \\(\\Gamma_{ij}\\) on \\(S\\times A_1\\times A_2\\). Their codimension is two, since the target correspondences have degree shift zero.</p><p>Lemma 6.3 and the spanning calculation show that the complex span of the \\(\\Phi_{ij}\\) is\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}}\n\\bigl(V_{\\mathbb C},\nW_{\\mathbb C}^{\\epsilon_1}\\otimes W_{\\mathbb C}^{\\epsilon_2}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe prescribed rational Hodge map \\(\\beta\\) lies in this space. Thus\n\\[\n\\beta=\\sum_{i=1}^r\\sum_{j=1}^s c_{ij}\\Phi_{ij}\n\\]\nhas a solution over \\(\\mathbb C\\). The system and right-hand side are rational, so Proposition 1 gives a solution with \\(c_{ij}\\in\\mathbb Q\\). Then\n\\[\n\\Gamma=\\sum_{i,j}c_{ij}\\Gamma_{ij}\n\\]\nhas the required action.</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nV(1)\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{Hom}(W^+,W^-),\n\\qquad x\\longmapsto L_x\n\\]\nis a rational Hodge map. A weight-one polarization identifies \\((W^+)^\\vee(-1)\\) with \\(W^+\\), making it a rational Hodge map \\(V\\to W^-\\otimes W^+\\). It is therefore realized by the preceding argument. Proposition 4 supplies the fixed odd right multiplier and prescribed duality.</p><p>As emphasized in original Remark 4.6, complex decompositions are used only to compute a span. The maps \\(f_i,g_j\\) and cycles \\(\\Gamma_{ij}\\) are rational before that computation. No split rational Clifford algebra or descent of an individual complex spin summand is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "The orthogonal ideal in the joint Hodge Lie algebra", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>In the setting of Proposition 6, let \\(\\mathcal H\\) be the joint Hodge group of \\(V,U,W^+,W^-\\), and let \\(\\mathfrak h\\) be its rational Lie algebra.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a decomposition into commuting rational ideals\n\\[\n\\mathfrak h=\\mathfrak g\\oplus\\mathfrak k,\n\\qquad\n\\mathfrak g\\simeq\\mathfrak{so}(V,q),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathfrak k\\) is the kernel of the action on \\(V\\). The ideal \\(\\mathfrak k\\) acts trivially on \\(W^+\\) and \\(W^-\\), and \\(\\mathfrak g\\) acts on them by the usual left spin action.</p>", "proof": "<p>The group \\(\\mathcal H\\) is connected by the circle argument used in Proposition 5. It is reductive. Each of the four pure summands is semisimple as a rational \\(\\mathcal H\\)-module: an invariant rational subspace is a Hodge substructure, and a polarization provides a rational Hodge complement. Their direct sum is a faithful semisimple module. The unipotent radical has nonzero invariants in each irreducible constituent; normality makes those invariants \\(\\mathcal H\\)-stable, so it acts trivially on every constituent. Faithfulness forces the radical to be trivial.</p><p>Projection to \\(V\\) maps \\(\\mathcal H\\) onto \\(\\mathrm{Hg}(V)=\\mathrm{SO}(V,q)\\). Its closed rational image contains the projected circle and therefore contains \\(\\mathrm{Hg}(V)\\). Conversely, the preimage of \\(\\mathrm{Hg}(V)\\) contains the joint circle, so minimality places \\(\\mathcal H\\) inside that preimage.</p><p>The reductive rational Lie algebra \\(\\mathfrak h\\) is the direct sum of its center and its rational simple ideals. Exactly one simple ideal maps nontrivially to the absolutely simple quotient \\(\\mathfrak{so}(V,q)\\): its image is the whole quotient, and the commuting images of all other ideals vanish. The nonzero map from that simple ideal is an isomorphism. This gives the asserted decomposition.</p><p>On \\(V\\oplus W^+\\oplus W^-\\), the image of \\(\\mathcal H\\) is contained in the rational algebraic image of \\(\\mathrm{Spin}(V,q)\\), acting on \\(V\\) through its orthogonal quotient and on \\(W^\\pm\\) by left multiplication. That image contains the projected Hodge circle, so the containment follows by minimality. The differential of\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Spin}(V,q)\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{SO}(V,q)\n\\]\nis an isomorphism. Its Lie algebra kernel is zero, proving the remaining assertions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "The spin representation forced by weight one", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the setting and decomposition of Lemma 6.1. Let \\(\\Delta\\) be the complex spin representation of \\(\\mathfrak{so}_{21}(\\mathbb C)\\), of dimension \\(2^{10}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are complex multiplicity spaces \\(M,N_+,N_-\\) and a trivial \\(\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}\\)-module \\(M_0\\) such that\n\\[\nU_{\\mathbb C}=(\\Delta\\otimes M)\\oplus M_0,\n\\qquad\nW^\\pm_{\\mathbb C}=\\Delta\\otimes N_\\pm,\n\\qquad\n\\dim N_\\pm=2^{10}.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nEvery rational \\(\\mathfrak g\\)-equivariant map \\(U\\to W^\\pm\\) is a weight-one Hodge map. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathrm{HS}}(U,W^\\pm)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb C\n=\n\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}}(U_{\\mathbb C},W^\\pm_{\\mathbb C})\n\\simeq\n\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathbb C}(M,N_\\pm).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(D\\) be the infinitesimal Hodge-circle generator divided by \\(i\\); on type \\((p,r)\\) its eigenvalue is \\(p-r\\). In the decomposition of Lemma 6.1, write\n\\[\nD=D_{\\mathfrak g}+D'.\n\\]\nOn \\(V_{\\mathbb C}\\), the eigenvalues are \\(2,-2,0,\\ldots,0\\). In the standard Cartan coordinates for type \\(B_{10}\\), with vector weights\n\\[\n\\pm e_1,\\ldots,\\pm e_{10},0,\n\\]\ntake\n\\[\nD_{\\mathfrak g}=2e_1^\\vee.\n\\]</p><p>The element \\(D_{\\mathfrak g}\\) is semisimple on every finite-dimensional \\(\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}\\)-module. It commutes with \\(D\\), so \\(D'=D-D_{\\mathfrak g}\\) is also semisimple on \\(U_{\\mathbb C}\\). Each \\(D'\\)-eigenspace is \\(\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}\\)-stable.</p><p>Consider an irreducible constituent in such an eigenspace, with highest weight\n\\[\n\\lambda=(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_{10}).\n\\]\nThe highest-weight description for \\(B_{10}\\) gives\n\\[\n\\lambda_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_{10}\\ge0,\n\\]\nwith either every \\(\\lambda_i\\in\\mathbb Z\\), or every \\(\\lambda_i\\in\\tfrac12+\\mathbb Z\\). The largest and smallest eigenvalues of \\(2e_1^\\vee\\) are \\(2\\lambda_1\\) and \\(-2\\lambda_1\\): \\(e_1^\\vee\\) is dominant, and the Weyl group permits every coordinate sign change.</p><p>Adding the scalar by which \\(D'\\) acts does not change the difference \\(4\\lambda_1\\) between the extreme eigenvalues. Since \\(U\\) has types \\((1,0),(0,1)\\), the eigenvalues of \\(D\\) belong to \\(\\{1,-1\\}\\). Hence \\(4\\lambda_1\\le2\\). The only possibilities are the trivial representation and\n\\[\n\\lambda=(\\tfrac12,\\ldots,\\tfrac12),\n\\]\nwhich is \\(\\Delta\\). On a spin constituent, the extreme eigenvalues are already \\(1,-1\\), and the only scalar shift preserving this pair is zero. Thus\n\\[\nD'=0\\quad\\text{on }\\Delta\\otimes M.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]</p><p>The complex Clifford identity\n\\[\nC^+(V_{\\mathbb C},q)\\simeq\\mathrm{End}_{\\mathbb C}(\\Delta),\n\\]\nwith left multiplication, gives the assertion for \\(W^+\\). An invertible odd right multiplier identifies the two parity spaces for the left spin action and gives the assertion for \\(W^-\\). Lemma 6.1 gives \\(D'=0\\) on both.</p><p>A rational \\(\\mathfrak g\\)-map \\(f:U\\to W^\\pm\\) vanishes on \\(M_0\\) after complexification. The summand \\(M_0\\) is \\(D\\)-stable. On the spin summand, \\((4.4)\\) shows that \\(f\\) intertwines \\(D\\). It therefore intertwines the Hodge circle and is a Hodge map. Conversely, every rational Hodge map intertwines \\(\\mathcal H\\) and hence \\(\\mathfrak g\\).</p><p>Finally, rational \\(\\mathfrak g\\)-intertwiners form the kernel of the rational linear equations\n\\[\nf\\rho_U(X_a)=\\rho_{W^\\pm}(X_a)f,\n\\]\nwhere \\((X_a)\\) is a rational basis of \\(\\mathfrak g\\). Taking this kernel commutes with scalar extension. Schur's lemma and \\((4.2)\\) give \\((4.3)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Vector multiplicity in the spin tensor square", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(V_{\\mathbb C}\\) be the standard \\(21\\)-dimensional representation of \\(\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}\\simeq\\mathfrak{so}_{21}(\\mathbb C)\\), and let \\(\\Delta\\) be its \\(2^{10}\\)-dimensional spin representation.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathfrak g_{\\mathbb C}}\n(V_{\\mathbb C},\\Delta\\otimes\\Delta)=1.\n\\]\nNeither \\(\\Delta\\) nor the trivial representation contains a copy of \\(V_{\\mathbb C}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The spin representation in type \\(B_{10}\\) is self-dual, since the longest Weyl element is \\(-1\\). Under the conjugation action, Clifford antisymmetrization gives\n\\[\n\\Delta\\otimes\\Delta\n\\simeq\\mathrm{End}(\\Delta)\n\\simeq C^+(V_{\\mathbb C},q)\n\\simeq\\bigoplus_{k\\ \\mathrm{even}}\\bigwedge\\nolimits^kV_{\\mathbb C}.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nThis is the conjugation action, unlike the left action in \\((4.2)\\).</p><p>Choose an orthonormal basis \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_{21}\\). An equivariant map\n\\[\nF:V_{\\mathbb C}\\to\\bigwedge\\nolimits^kV_{\\mathbb C}\n\\]\nis determined by \\(F(v_1)\\), because the orthogonal orbit of \\(v_1\\) spans \\(V_{\\mathbb C}\\). This value is fixed by all determinant-one diagonal sign changes on \\(v_2,\\ldots,v_{21}\\). A fixed basis exterior monomial contains either none or all of these twenty vectors. For even \\(k\\), only \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=20\\) can occur.</p><p>The degree-zero map vanishes: a determinant-one orthogonal transformation sending \\(v_1\\) to \\(-v_1\\) forces\n\\[\nF(v_1)=F(-v_1)=-F(v_1).\n\\]\nFor \\(k=20\\), the value is a multiple of \\(v_2\\wedge\\cdots\\wedge v_{21}\\), and volume duality\n\\[\n\\bigwedge\\nolimits^{20}V_{\\mathbb C}\\simeq V_{\\mathbb C}\n\\]\nsupplies a nonzero equivariant map. The multiplicity is exactly one.</p><p>The last assertion follows from irreducibility and the distinct dimensions \\(21,2^{10},1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Specialization from very general algebraicity", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a connected polydisc.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p:\\mathcal X\\to D\\) be a smooth proper holomorphic family of \\(n\\)-dimensional varieties, \\(n\\ge2\\), with a relative embedding\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal X\\hookrightarrow D\\times\\mathbb P^N.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\theta\\in H^0(D,R^4p_*\\mathbb Q)\n  \\]\n  be a flat rational class.</p><p class=\"li\">“Very general” means outside a countable union of proper closed analytic subsets.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\theta_t\\) is the class of a rational codimension-two algebraic cycle for very general \\(t\\in D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(t\\in D\\), the class \\(\\theta_t\\) is the class of a rational codimension-two algebraic cycle.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix a desired point \\(0\\in D\\), and trivialize \\(R^4p_*\\mathbb Q\\). By Lemma 7.1 write the family as the pullback of \\(\\mathcal U\\to H\\) along \\(\\eta:D\\to H^{\\mathrm{an}}\\).</p><p>For each possible Hilbert polynomial \\(Q\\) of degree \\(n-2\\), consider\n\\[\nT_Q=\\mathrm{Hilb}^Q(\\mathcal U/H).\n\\]\nIt is projective over \\(H\\), has a flat universal subscheme, and has finitely many reduced irreducible components. Resolve each component by a projective birational morphism, obtaining finitely many smooth irreducible varieties\n\\[\nR\\longrightarrow(T_Q)_{\\mathrm{red}}\n\\]\nwhose images cover \\((T_Q)_{\\mathrm{red}}\\). Each composite \\(R\\to H\\) is proper. Pullback gives a smooth projective ambient family \\(\\mathcal U_R\\to R\\) and a flat closed subscheme \\(Z_R\\subset\\mathcal U_R\\). By Lemma 7.2, their fiber top-cycle classes are sections of the degree-four cohomology local system.</p><p>Form\n\\[\nK_R=D\\times_{H^{\\mathrm{an}}}R^{\\mathrm{an}}.\n\\]\nThese spaces are proper over \\(D\\). Their ambient families agree with the pullback of \\(\\mathcal X\\), so the cycle classes become locally constant functions on \\(K_R\\) with values in the fixed marked space \\(H^4(\\mathcal X_0,\\mathbb Q)\\). Singularities or nonreduced structure on \\(K_R\\) cause no difficulty: local constancy was established on the smooth parameter \\(R\\) before base change.</p><p>There are countably many \\(Q\\) and finitely many choices of \\(R\\) for each \\(Q\\). Each \\(K_R\\) is second countable and has a locally finite family of closed irreducible analytic components, hence at most countably many. Every component is proper over \\(D\\); Remmert's Proper Mapping Theorem makes its image a closed analytic subset. Let \\(E\\) be the union of images of components whose image is not all of \\(D\\). It is a countable union of proper closed analytic subsets.</p><p>Choose \\(t\\in D\\) outside \\(E\\) and the exceptional sets in the hypothesis. Such a point exists by the Baire Category Theorem. Choose an actual cycle representing \\(\\theta_t\\):\n\\[\n\\gamma_t=\\sum_{j=1}^s a_j[Y_{j,t}],\n\\qquad a_j\\in\\mathbb Q,\n\\]\nwhere each \\(Y_{j,t}\\subset\\mathcal X_t\\) is integral of codimension two.</p><p>Each Hilbert point lifts to one of the parameters \\(R\\), hence to \\(u_j\\in K_R\\) above \\(t\\). Choose an irreducible component \\(C_j\\) containing \\(u_j\\). Since \\(t\\notin E\\), the map \\(C_j\\to D\\) is surjective, so there is \\(v_j\\in C_j\\) above \\(0\\).</p><p>An irreducible complex analytic space is connected. The marked cycle class is constant on \\(C_j\\). The fiber of the universal subscheme at \\(v_j\\) has top cycle \\(\\delta_{j,0}\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\mathrm{cl}(\\delta_{j,0})\n=\n\\mathrm{cl}([Y_{j,t}])\n\\]\nunder the marking. These are algebraic cycles on \\(\\mathcal X_0\\): the universal-subscheme fibers are algebraic in \\(\\mathbb P^N\\), and the embedded fiber identification is algebraic by GAGA. Therefore\n\\[\n\\gamma_0=\\sum_{j=1}^s a_j\\delta_{j,0},\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{cl}(\\gamma_0)=\\theta_0.\n\\]\nThe argument applies to every chosen point.</p><p>The avoidance is essential: density of algebraic fibers alone would not ensure that cycle-parameter components reach the desired fiber. The proposition uses actual very-general algebraicity, not a principle deriving algebraicity from the Hodge property of a flat class.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "An analytic classifying map", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(p:\\mathcal X\\to D\\) be a smooth proper holomorphic family of nonempty, pure \\(n\\)-dimensional varieties over a connected complex manifold, with an embedding\n\\[\n\\mathcal X\\hookrightarrow D\\times\\mathbb P^N\n\\]\nover \\(D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a reduced complex quasi-projective scheme \\(H\\), a smooth projective algebraic family \\(\\mathcal U\\to H\\), and a holomorphic map\n\\[\n\\eta:D\\to H^{\\mathrm{an}}\n\\]\nsuch that \\(\\mathcal X\\) is the embedded analytic pullback of \\(\\mathcal U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The embedded fibers have a common Hilbert polynomial \\(P\\). Riemann–Roch expresses\n\\[\n\\chi(\\mathcal X_t,\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal X_t}(k))\n\\]\nin terms of fiber integrals of Chern classes of the relative tangent bundle and hyperplane bundle. These integrals are locally constant in a smooth proper family, and \\(D\\) is connected.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathrm{Hilb}^P(\\mathbb P^N)\\) be the algebraic Hilbert scheme. Fixed-polynomial boundedness supplies one sufficiently large \\(k\\) such that every member \\(Y\\) has\n\\[\n\\dim\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(\nH^0(\\mathbb P^N,\\mathcal O(k))\n\\to H^0(Y,\\mathcal O_Y(k))\n\\bigr)=P(k),\n\\]\nand its degree-\\(k\\) ideal space defines the Hilbert scheme's closed embedding in the corresponding Grassmannian [8, Theorem 3.2 and Proposition 3.8].</p><p>Fix \\(t_0\\in D\\). Since \\(\\mathcal X_{t_0}\\) is reduced, point evaluations separate restrictions of degree-\\(k\\) polynomials. Choose finitely many points whose evaluations have rank \\(P(k)\\), and extend them to local holomorphic sections of \\(p\\), using its submersion property. After locally trivializing \\(\\mathcal O(k)\\) along these sections, evaluation is a holomorphic matrix. A nonzero \\(P(k)\\)-minor remains nonzero near \\(t_0\\). Its rank cannot exceed \\(P(k)\\), because evaluation factors through restriction to \\(\\mathcal X_t\\). Its kernel therefore equals\n\\[\nH^0(\\mathbb P^N,\\mathcal I_{\\mathcal X_t}(k)).\n\\]\nThese kernels give a holomorphic Grassmannian map.</p><p>Pointwise the map lies in the Hilbert locus. Since \\(D\\) is reduced, the defining equations of that locus vanish on \\(D\\), so the map factors holomorphically through the Hilbert scheme.</p><p>Take \\(H\\) to be the reduction of the open locus parametrizing smooth pure \\(n\\)-dimensional subschemes, and \\(\\mathcal U\\) the restricted universal family. Flatness and smoothness of all fibers imply that \\(\\mathcal U\\to H\\) is smooth. Its pullback and \\(\\mathcal X\\) are reduced closed analytic subspaces of \\(D\\times\\mathbb P^N\\) with the same fibers and underlying set. They coincide.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Classes of flat subschemes", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f:M\\to R\\) be a smooth projective algebraic family of relative dimension \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R\\) be a smooth irreducible complex variety of dimension \\(b\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Z\\subset M\\) be a closed subscheme, flat over \\(R\\), whose fibers have dimension \\(n-2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a closed subscheme \\(Z'\\) of dimension at most \\(a\\), write \\([Z']_a\\) for its \\(a\\)-dimensional fundamental cycle, with generic lengths as multiplicities. Smaller-dimensional components do not enter \\([Z']_a\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The assignment\n\\[\nr\\longmapsto\\mathrm{cl}([Z_r]_{n-2})\\in H^4(M_r,\\mathbb Q)\n\\]\nis a section of \\(R^4f_*^{\\mathrm{an}}\\mathbb Q\\). This includes nonpure and nonreduced fibers.</p>", "proof": "<p>The total space \\(M\\) is smooth. Flatness and the dimension formula give \\(\\dim Z=n+b-2\\), so its top cycle has a class\n\\[\n\\zeta=\\mathrm{cl}([Z]_{n+b-2})\\in H^4(M,\\mathbb Q).\n\\]\nThe usual cycle class on a smooth variety takes values in ordinary cohomology; completeness of \\(M\\) is unnecessary.</p><p>For \\(i_r:M_r\\hookrightarrow M\\), claim\n\\[\ni_r^*\\zeta=\\mathrm{cl}([Z_r]_{n-2}).\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nChoose local algebraic coordinates by an étale map from a neighborhood \\(U\\subset R\\) to \\(\\mathbb A^b\\), taking \\(r\\) to \\(0\\). Shrink \\(U\\) to remove the other points over \\(0\\). The coordinate functions cut out a smooth flag\n\\[\nU=U_0\\supset U_1\\supset\\cdots\\supset U_b=\\{r\\}.\n\\]\nPut \\(Z_j=Z\\times_RU_j\\). It is flat over \\(U_j\\) and has dimension at most \\(n+b-2-j\\). At each step the next coordinate is a nonzerodivisor on \\(U_j\\), hence on the entire scheme \\(Z_j\\). The Cartier intersection formula gives\n\\[\n[Z_j]_{n+b-2-j}\n\\longmapsto\n[Z_{j+1}]_{n+b-3-j}\n\\]\nwith scheme multiplicities [15, Lemma 42.29.5, Tag 02TB]. Successive application yields\n\\[\ni_r^![Z]_{n+b-2}=[Z_r]_{n-2}.\n\\]\nThe formula applies to the whole possibly nonpure or nonreduced scheme at every cut; no componentwise flatness is asserted. Compatibility of Betti cycle classes with regular pullback proves \\((5.1)\\).</p><p>Restrictions of a single cohomology class on the total space of a smooth proper family form a section of its cohomology local system, directly by local differentiable triviality. Apply this to \\(\\zeta\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Local polarized families", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a primitively amply polarized K3 surface \\((S_0,h)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After choosing a sufficiently small full-dimensional polarized period polydisc \\(D\\) about its period, there are smooth proper holomorphic families\n\\[\n\\mathcal S\\to D,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal A\\to D\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li\">the K3 family has central fiber \\((S_0,h)\\), a relative ample line bundle, and a marking of its integral second cohomology;</p><p class=\"li\">its primitive rational local system is a fixed quadratic space \\((V,q)\\) of dimension \\(21\\);</p><p class=\"li\">the abelian family has marked rational first cohomology\n  \\[\n  W=C^+(V,q)\n  \\]\n  with the full Clifford Hodge structures;</p><p class=\"li\">fixed rational polarization and parity-identification data give a flat family of standard Hodge tensor maps\n  \\[\n  \\mu_t:V\\longrightarrow H^1(A_t,\\mathbb Q)\\otimes H^1(A_t,\\mathbb Q);\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">after shrinking \\(D\\),\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal X=\\mathcal S\\times_D\\mathcal A\\times_D\\mathcal A\n  \\]\n  admits a relative embedding in \\(D\\times\\mathbb P^N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Polarized deformation theory and local Torelli give a smooth K3 family over a \\(19\\)-dimensional polydisc on which the period map is a local isomorphism [10, Chapter 6, Section 2]. Contractibility of \\(D\\) marks its cohomology local systems.</p><p>The class \\(h\\) remains of type \\((1,1)\\). Local differentiable triviality over the contractible base extends it to \\(H^2(\\mathcal S,\\mathbb Z)\\). Since \\(D\\) is Stein, coherent Leray and Cartan's Theorem B identify\n\\[\nH^2(\\mathcal S,\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal S})\n=\nH^0(D,R^2p_*\\mathcal O_{\\mathcal S}),\n\\qquad p:\\mathcal S\\to D.\n\\]\nThe right-hand direct image is locally free and commutes with base change. The image of \\(h\\) vanishes fiberwise and therefore vanishes. The exponential sequence gives a holomorphic line bundle \\(\\mathcal L\\) with first Chern class \\(h\\).</p><p>A high power of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is very ample on \\(S_0\\) and has vanishing higher cohomology there. Proper cohomology and base change extend a basis of sections near \\(0\\). These give a relative embedding after shrinking. Basepoint freeness and immersion persist near the central fiber, and properness excludes distinct points with the same image: a sequence of such pairs approaching the central fiber either limits to distinct points, contradicting its embedding, or to one point, contradicting the local embedding supplied by immersion. Thus the family is locally relatively projective, and \\(h\\) remains ample.</p><p>On the fixed Clifford space \\(W\\), use the holomorphic filtration and fixed rational alternating polarization of Proposition 3. If \\(\\omega_t\\) spans the period line, then\n\\[\nF^1W_{\\mathbb C}\n=\n\\operatorname{im}\\bigl(\nL_{\\omega_t}:C^-(V_{\\mathbb C},q)\\to C^+(V_{\\mathbb C},q)\n\\bigr).\n\\]\nThis has constant half dimension and varies holomorphically. The polarization is fixed on a sufficiently small chart.</p><p>Choose a rational symplectic basis, and let \\(\\Lambda\\) be its integral span in \\(W\\). It is unimodular for the chosen symplectic form and does not change the rational Hodge structure. Since \\(W\\) is cohomology, the corresponding torus is\n\\[\nA_t=(F^1W_t)^\\vee/\\operatorname{image}(\\Lambda^\\vee),\n\\]\nwhere a lattice functional is restricted to \\(F^1W_t\\). Thus\n\\[\nH_1(A_t,\\mathbb Z)=\\Lambda^\\vee,\n\\qquad\nH^1(A_t,\\mathbb Z)=\\Lambda,\n\\qquad\nH^{1,0}(A_t)=F^1W_t.\n\\]\nThe polarization gives the Riemann form on the dual lattice. Equivalently, the marked filtrations define a holomorphic map to Siegel space, and \\(\\mathcal A\\to D\\) is the pullback of its marked torus family. Full markings give level-\\(3\\) data. The spin action is defined on the fixed Clifford space over \\(D\\), so there is no monodromy lifting problem.</p><p>In symplectic period coordinates,\n\\[\nA_t=\\mathbb C^g/(\\mathbb Z^g+\\tau(t)\\mathbb Z^g),\n\\qquad\n\\tau(t)^{\\mathsf t}=\\tau(t),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Im}\\tau(t)&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe factors\n\\[\n\\exp\\bigl(-\\pi i\\,n^{\\mathsf t}\\tau(t)n-2\\pi i\\,n^{\\mathsf t}z\\bigr),\n\\qquad\nm+\\tau(t)n\\in\\mathbb Z^g+\\tau(t)\\mathbb Z^g,\n\\]\ndefine a holomorphic theta line bundle, ample on each fiber. The extension-of-sections argument gives a relative projective embedding after shrinking \\(D\\).</p><p>Fix a rational nonisotropic \\(z_0\\in V\\) and the polarization identification\n\\[\nj:W^\\vee(-1)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}W.\n\\]\nThe maps\n\\[\nV(1)\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{End}(W),\n\\qquad\nx\\longmapsto[w\\longmapsto xwz_0]\n\\]\ngive \\(\\mu_t\\) after untwisting and applying \\(j\\) to the dual slot. Clifford multiplication, \\(z_0\\), and \\(j\\) are constant rational data in the markings. Thus \\(\\mu_t\\) is flat and is a Hodge map in every fiber.</p><p>A Segre embedding combines the relative embeddings of \\(\\mathcal S\\) and the two copies of \\(\\mathcal A\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "The primitive map at every polarized fiber", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Fix a polarized moduli component and assume that the nonzero algebraic correspondence input of Theorem 1(i) holds at very general points.</p><p>Alternatively, assume that, about each desired point, it holds outside countably many proper closed analytic subsets in a full-dimensional polarized period chart.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At every \\((S_0,h)\\) under consideration, the standard full primitive Clifford tensor for \\(V=h^\\perp\\) and the cup form \\(q\\) is induced by a rational codimension-two algebraic cycle. Clifford multiplication \\(m^q\\) itself is algebraically realized.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the families of Proposition 8 about \\((S_0,h)\\). Under the markings, extend \\(\\mu_t\\) by zero on \\(\\mathbb Qh\\) and the other degrees of \\(H^*(S_t,\\mathbb Q)\\). Poincaré duality and Künneth give a unique flat class\n\\[\n\\theta\\in H^0\\bigl(D,R^4(\\mathcal X/D)_*\\mathbb Q\\bigr)\n\\]\nwith this action. For a rational basis \\((v_i)\\) of \\(V\\), with \\((q^{ij})\\) the inverse cup-pairing matrix,\n\\[\n\\theta_t\n=\n\\sum_{i,j}q^{ij}v_i\\otimes\\mu_t(v_j)\n\\in\nV\\otimes\\bigl(H^1(A_t)\\otimes H^1(A_t)\\bigr)\n\\subset H^4(\\mathcal X_t,\\mathbb Q).\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]</p><p>By Proposition 5, after excluding countably many proper closed analytic subsets,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hg}(V_t)=\\mathrm{SO}(V,q).\n\\]\nAt a point satisfying this and the input hypothesis, Proposition 6 produces a cycle inducing \\(\\mu_t\\) on \\(V_t\\).</p><p>Its full class can be made exactly \\(\\theta_t\\). On \\(S_t\\), the algebraic correspondence\n\\[\n\\pi_{V,t}\n=\n[\\Delta_{S_t}]\n-\n[o_t]\\times S_t\n-\nS_t\\times[o_t]\n-\n\\frac{h\\times h}{\\int_{S_t}h^2},\n\\qquad o_t\\in S_t,\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nacts identically on \\(V_t\\) and kills \\(H^0,H^4,\\mathbb Qh\\). K3 surfaces have no odd cohomology. Precompose the cycle with \\(\\pi_{V,t}\\), and postcompose with the algebraic projector onto\n\\[\nH^1(A_t)\\otimes H^1(A_t)\n\\]\nfrom Proposition 1. These operations preserve codimension two. The cycle now has the prescribed action in every source degree, so Poincaré duality and Künneth identify its entire class with \\((5.2)\\). The point \\(o_t\\) is needed only on that fiber; its cohomology class and the projector's cohomological action are canonical.</p><p>If the hypothesis is given in algebraic moduli, its exceptional loci pull back to proper closed analytic subsets of \\(D\\). They remain proper because the full-dimensional polarized period chart has locally open image in the component, up to a finite automorphism quotient. Hence \\(\\theta_t\\) is algebraic very generally in either version of the hypothesis.</p><p>Proposition 7 gives an actual rational codimension-two cycle with class \\(\\theta_0\\). Its action on \\(V\\) is exactly \\(\\mu_0\\). Undoing the fixed invertible right multiplier on the output Hom slot by Proposition 4 also realizes \\(m^q\\).</p><p>The original witnesses need not form a family. Proposition 6 first transfers their input to the fixed local full Clifford family, and specialization uses a finite representative of one sufficiently general cycle with its rational coefficients unchanged.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Restriction to the transcendental part", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\((S,h)\\) be any primitively amply polarized K3 surface, and put\n\\[\nV=h^\\perp\\subset H^2(S,\\mathbb Q),\n\\qquad\nT=\\mathrm{NS}(S)_{\\mathbb Q}^{\\perp}\\subset V,\n\\qquad\nq_0=q|_T.\n\\]\nAssume the primitive Clifford multiplication \\(m^q\\) on \\(V\\) is algebraically realized. No full Hodge-group hypothesis is imposed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The Clifford multiplication \\(m^{q_0}\\) on \\(T\\) is algebraically realized.</p>", "proof": "<p>The orthogonal decomposition\n\\[\nV=T\\oplus T^\\perp\n\\]\nis a decomposition of rational Hodge structures, and \\(T\\) contains the period plane. The Clifford Basis Theorem gives inclusions\n\\[\nC^\\pm(T,q_0)\\hookrightarrow C^\\pm(V,q).\n\\]\nThey are weight-one Hodge inclusions because the element \\(J\\) defining the left Hodge circle already belongs to \\(C^+(T_{\\mathbb R},q_0)\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\ni:C^+(T,q_0)\\to C^+(V,q)\n\\]\nbe the inclusion, and choose a rational Hodge retraction\n\\[\np:C^-(V,q)\\to C^-(T,q_0).\n\\]\nIt exists by polarizability: the orthogonal complement for a weight-one polarization is a rational Hodge complement.</p><p>For every \\(x\\in T\\),\n\\[\npL_x^Vi=L_x^T,\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nbecause \\(xC^+(T,q_0)\\subset C^-(T,q_0)\\) and \\(p\\) is the identity there. Proposition 4 realizes the two operations on the abelian factors. Restricting the resulting cycle action to \\(T\\) proves the assertion. No operation on the K3 factor is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Change of quadratic polarization", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(T\\subset H^2(S,\\mathbb Q)\\) be a rational Hodge structure of K3 type on a smooth projective surface \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(q_0,q_1\\) be rational quadratic Hodge polarizations on \\(T\\), allowing either overall sign as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(m^{q_0}\\) is algebraically realized, in the precise tensor sense of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The multiplication \\(m^{q_1}\\) is algebraically realized.</p><p>The construction uses operations on the abelian factors and rational descent; it does not require algebraic correspondences on \\(S\\) representing the Hodge endomorphisms used to compare the two forms. It applies in particular to transcendental ranks \\(2,3,4\\), and to the presence of additional real or complex multiplication.</p>", "proof": "<p>First construct square-root isometries. Write\n\\[\nq_1(u,v)=q_0(Pu,v).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nThe form isomorphisms from \\(T\\) to its twisted dual show that \\(P\\) is an invertible rational Hodge endomorphism. Symmetry makes \\(P\\) self-adjoint for \\(q_0\\).</p><p>Let \\(K\\) be \\(+1\\) on the real period plane and \\(-1\\) on the real \\((1,1)\\)-part, and let \\(\\epsilon_0\\) be the sign of \\(q_0\\) on the period plane. Then\n\\[\n(u,v)_0=\\epsilon_0q_0(Ku,v)\n\\]\nis positive definite. Since \\(P\\) commutes with \\(K\\), it is self-adjoint for this positive definite real form. Hence it is semisimple with real nonzero eigenvalues. Its distinct eigenvalues\n\\[\n\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_r\n\\]\nand spectral projectors\n\\[\np_1,\\ldots,p_r\n\\]\nare defined over \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\), with the projectors polynomial in \\(P\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\nu_i\\in\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\) such that \\(\\nu_i^2=\\lambda_i\\). For\n\\[\ns=(s_1,\\ldots,s_r)\\in\\{1,-1\\}^r,\n\\]\nput\n\\[\nR_s=\\sum_{i=1}^rs_i\\nu_ip_i.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nThese are invertible self-adjoint operators with \\(R_s^2=P\\). Therefore\n\\[\nq_0(R_su,R_sv)=q_0(Pu,v)=q_1(u,v).\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nR_s:(T,q_1)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\n\\longrightarrow(T,q_0)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\n\\]\nis an isometry and extends to a parity-preserving Clifford algebra isomorphism\n\\[\nF_s:C(T,q_1)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\nC(T,q_0)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}.\n\\]</p><p>Next show that the parity restrictions of \\(F_s\\) and their inverses lie in the \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\)-span of rational weight-one Hodge maps.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal J\\) be the joint rational Hodge group of \\(T\\) and\n\\[\nP_j^\\pm=C^\\pm(T,q_j),\n\\qquad j=0,1.\n\\]\nIts geometric identity component is defined over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and contains the connected simultaneous Hodge circle. Minimality gives \\(\\mathcal J=\\mathcal J^\\circ\\), so \\(\\mathcal J\\) is geometrically connected.</p><p>Write \\(\\rho_T\\) for its action on \\(T\\). Since \\(P\\) is a rational Hodge endomorphism, it commutes with \\(\\rho_T(\\mathcal J)\\). Every \\(R_s\\) also commutes with this action after scalar extension.</p><p>For each \\(j\\), projection of \\(\\mathcal J\\) to the actions on \\(T,P_j^+,P_j^-\\) lies in the image of\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Spin}(T,q_j)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\mathrm{GL}(T)\\times\\mathrm{GL}(P_j^+)\\times\\mathrm{GL}(P_j^-).\n\\]\nThis rational algebraic image contains the projected circle by \\((3.1)\\). The representation is faithful, since evaluation at \\(1\\) in the left action on \\(C^+\\) recovers a spin element. It identifies the spin group with its algebraic image and gives algebraic homomorphisms\n\\[\n\\rho_j:\\mathcal J\\longrightarrow\\mathrm{Spin}(T,q_j)\n\\]\ncovering the same action \\(\\rho_T\\).</p><p>The isometry \\(R_s\\) induces\n\\[\n\\widehat R_s:\n\\mathrm{Spin}(T,q_1)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\n\\longrightarrow\n\\mathrm{Spin}(T,q_0)_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(R_s\\) commutes with \\(\\rho_T\\), both \\(\\widehat R_s\\rho_1\\) and \\(\\rho_0\\) lift the same orthogonal representation of \\(\\mathcal J_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}\\). Their pointwise quotient takes values in the central kernel \\(\\mu_2\\). It is a morphism from a connected group to a finite group and is the identity at the identity. Hence\n\\[\n\\widehat R_s\\rho_1=\\rho_0.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nSince \\(F_s\\) is an algebra isomorphism, this means that it intertwines the left \\(\\mathcal J\\)-actions on both parities.</p><p>Equivariant Hom-spaces for finite-dimensional rational representations commute with field extension: equivariance is a set of rational linear equations. Rational maps intertwine \\(\\mathcal J\\) exactly when they intertwine its Hodge circle. Thus\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathcal J}(P_1^\\pm,P_0^\\pm)\n\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\n=\n\\mathrm{Hom}_{\\mathcal J_{\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}}\n(P_{1,\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}^\\pm,P_{0,\\overline{\\mathbb Q}}^\\pm).\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nConsequently \\(F_s^\\pm\\) and \\((F_s^\\pm)^{-1}\\) are finite linear combinations of rational weight-one Hodge maps.</p><p>Finally recover the unmodified multiplication and descend rationally. Fix abelian realizations and rational dualities for the \\(q_1\\) target. Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be the rational subspace of maps\n\\[\nT\\longrightarrow P_1^-\\otimes P_1^+\n\\]\nobtained by restricting rational codimension-two cycle actions from \\(S\\) and projecting to this Künneth component.</p><p>By Proposition 4 and \\((6.5)\\), conjugation of the algebraically realized \\(q_0\\) multiplication on its Hom slots gives elements of \\(\\mathcal A\\otimes\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\). The exact Clifford identity is\n\\[\n(F_s^-)^{-1}L_x^{q_0}F_s^+\n=\nL_{R_s^{-1}x}^{q_1}\n\\qquad(x\\in T).\n\\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nThis conjugation is expanded using rational Hodge maps on the two abelian factors. It does not apply \\(R_s^{-1}\\) to the cohomology of \\(S\\) by a correspondence.</p><p>The sign average\n\\[\np_i=\n\\frac{\\nu_i}{2^r}\n\\sum_{s\\in\\{1,-1\\}^r}s_iR_s^{-1},\n\\qquad\n\\mathrm{id}_T=\\sum_{i=1}^rp_i\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nrecovers the unmodified source. Indeed,\n\\[\nR_s^{-1}=\\sum_j s_j\\nu_j^{-1}p_j,\n\\qquad\n\\sum_s s_is_j=2^r\\delta_{ij}.\n\\]\nEquations \\((6.6)\\) and \\((6.7)\\) place the tensor realization of \\(x\\mapsto L_x^{q_1}\\) in \\(\\mathcal A\\otimes\\overline{\\mathbb Q}\\). This map is rational, so Proposition 1 places it in \\(\\mathcal A\\). A finite rational linear combination of actual cycles realizes it.</p><p>For the scope recorded in original Remark 6.3, none of \\(P,p_i,R_s\\) is represented by a correspondence on the K3 surface. They enter only the identities producing weight-one intertwiners and finite rational descent. The endomorphism \\(P\\) need not be scalar, and no description of the special Hodge group is needed.</p><p>In rank two, the full even-Clifford space is two-dimensional, and\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Spin}_2\\to\\mathrm{SO}_2\n\\]\nis, over an algebraic closure, the squaring map on \\(\\mathbb G_m\\) with kernel \\(\\mu_2\\). The faithful left action and connected-lift argument remain valid. Ranks three and four introduce no exception.</p><p>For the opposite-sign rank-two example, take\n\\[\nq_0=\\operatorname{diag}(1,1),\n\\qquad q_1=-q_0\n\\]\nin the same period basis. Then \\(J_1=-e_1e_2\\), and the isometry \\(R=i\\,\\mathrm{id}\\) induces\n\\[\nF(J_1)=-(ie_1)(ie_2)=J_0.\n\\]\nThus the nonreal square root preserves the specified weight-one circle.</p><p>A rational Hodge automorphism \\(D\\) of \\(T\\) in a prescribed identification can also be incorporated without representing it on the K3 surface. First apply the proposition to the pullback form \\(D^*q_1\\). The rational isometry\n\\[\nD:(T,D^*q_1)\\to(T,q_1)\n\\]\ninduces weight-one Hodge isomorphisms on Clifford spaces by the same connected-lift argument, since \\(D\\) commutes with the joint Hodge action. Transport on the two Hom slots then gives exactly \\(x\\mapsto L_{Dx}^{q_1}\\).</p><p>The proposition does not assert that all Hodge embeddings for a smaller special Hodge group are generated by these operations.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 19, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 11, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [3, 21, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 0, "cite"], [7, 1, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [8, 21, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [9, 1, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [9, 19, "cite"], [9, 20, "cite"], [9, 21, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [10, 12, "cite"], [10, 19, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 1, "cite"], [20, 1, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 1, "cite"]]});
